TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "Lineup composition",
    "Player structural roles",
    "Organizer role",
    "Shaper role",
    "Terminal role",
    "Within-team variation",
    "Separability from talent",
    "Talent control",
    "Influence distribution in lineups"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How are player structural roles defined and assigned?",
    "How is lineup coverage operationalized using structural roles?",
    "Methods for assessing within-team variation in lineup composition by structural roles?",
    "How to measure player talent or general skill level?",
    "How to control for player talent or simple player counts when analyzing structural roles?",
    "How to demonstrate separability of structural role effects from talent/counts?",
    "How is 'talent control' or 'influence' defined and measured in a lineup?",
    "What statistical methods are suitable for examining the behavior of talent control in relation to lineup composition?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "No historical evidence was available to establish any findings or limitations regarding this research question or similar concepts within this project context."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The research question implies that player structural roles (organizer, shaper, terminal) can be reliably assigned to players based on the 'derived play-type possession records'.",
    "The concept of 'talent' needs to be operationalized using available per-game statistics or derived efficiency metrics.",
    "The phrase 'talent control behaves' requires precise operationalization, likely referring to how talent distribution or impact within a lineup changes with different structural role coverages."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definitions and assignment methodology for 'organizer, shaper, terminal' roles are not specified, which is critical for the study's validity.",
    "The exact meaning and operationalization of 'talent control behaves' is currently vague and needs clear definition.",
    "The specific measures for 'talent' are not defined; options exist using available data, but a choice must be made."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available for this research question, so it did not change the decision. The research remains an exploratory endeavor within the project context."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.8,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "The reliability and validity of the assignment of 'organizer, shaper, terminal' roles to players, derived from play-type data, are fundamental assumptions not explicitly detailed or verified. If these roles are not robustly defined or consistently assigned, the entire premise is at risk."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Player talent is likely highly correlated with the structural roles they assume or are assigned. Separating the independent effects of structural role coverage from the aggregate talent level or specific player types in a lineup will be challenging and prone to confounding."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "While the overall dataset is large, specific combinations of structural role coverage (e.g., highly specialized or unusual lineups) may occur infrequently, leading to sparse data for certain configurations and limiting statistical power for those specific cases."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No prior work was retrieved that directly addresses this research question within the project's historical records."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The quality and consistency of the 'organizer, shaper, terminal' role assignments derived from the play-type database are paramount. An unreliable or inconsistent assignment process would invalidate findings."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Distinguishing the impact of structural role coverage from that of inherent player talent and traditional positional counts is a significant challenge. High-talent players often naturally gravitate towards or are placed in key structural roles, making disentanglement difficult."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Certain specific lineup combinations of structural roles might be rare across the dataset, limiting the ability to draw robust conclusions about their effects due to insufficient sample size."
    },
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "If the operationalization of 'talent control' inadvertently incorporates elements of the outcomes it is meant to explain or describe, it could lead to tautological findings. Care must be taken to define this concept independently."
    },
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "Despite a large total number of possessions, the power to detect effects for specific, nuanced differences in lineup structural role coverage, especially after controlling for multiple confounding factors, might be limited, particularly for less common lineup types."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.4,
    "rejected": 0.2,
    "untestable": 0.1,
    "artifact": 0.1,
    "uncertain": 0.2
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "1. For 'Lineup coverage varies within team': Quantify the within-team variance and distribution of structural role coverage (e.g., number of players categorized as Organizer, Shaper, Terminal) across distinct lineups (defined by 5 players on court) within each team-season. 2. For 'separable from talent/counts': Estimate the partial effect of structural role coverage on lineup-level offensive and defensive efficiency metrics, after controlling for aggregate player talent (e.g., average individual offensive/defensive rating) and traditional player count (e.g., number of guards/forwards/centers) within the lineup. 3. For 'talent control behaves': Estimate how measures of talent distribution (e.g., variance of individual player offensive ratings within a lineup) or the effectiveness of high-talent players are moderated by different structural role coverages.",
    "unit": "The primary unit of analysis will be the `lineup-stint`, defined as a contiguous period of play by a fixed set of 5 players. Each `lineup-stint` will be aggregated to a `lineup-possession` level for outcome metrics. Team-season will be the grouping variable for within-team analysis.",
    "sample": "All 420,993 possessions from the NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (regular season and playoffs) will be used. Lineup stints will be filtered to include only those with a minimum duration (e.g., >=5 possessions) to ensure sufficient observation for stable metrics. Data will be aggregated to lineup-stint level for analysis.",
    "power": "Power analysis will be conducted post-hoc if significant effects are not found, or pre-hoc if specific effect sizes are hypothesized. Given 420,993 possessions and 90 team-seasons, there is likely adequate power for common lineup types and broad structural role categories. Sparse lineup configurations will be noted as limitations.",
    "controls": "To test separability, statistical models (e.g., mixed-effects regression) will control for: 1. Aggregate lineup talent (e.g., sum or average of individual player offensive/defensive ratings, or a composite talent metric). 2. Traditional positional player counts (e.g., number of guards, forwards, centers) in the lineup. 3. Opponent strength. 4. Game context variables (e.g., score differential, quarter, home/away). Team and season fixed effects will be included to account for unobserved heterogeneity.",
    "falsification": "The hypothesis for 'separability' would be falsified if, after controlling for talent and counts, structural role coverage shows no statistically significant additional explanatory power for lineup outcomes. For 'talent control behaves', falsification would occur if no discernible pattern or moderation effect of talent distribution by structural roles is observed.",
    "multiplicity": "Multiple tests will arise from examining different lineup outcomes (offensive/defensive efficiency) and various ways to define 'talent control'. A Bonferroni correction or False Discovery Rate (FDR) adjustment will be applied to p-values to control for Type I error across related tests. Clear primary outcomes and hypotheses will be pre-specified.",
    "decision_rule": "The research question will be considered 'supported' if statistically significant evidence (p < 0.05 after multiplicity adjustment) is found for within-team variation, separability of structural role effects from talent/counts, and a defined 'behavior' of talent control that aligns with a priori hypotheses. If evidence is inconclusive or effects are not significant, it will be deemed 'uncertain' or 'rejected' depending on power.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 (complete as of 2026-06-21 for playoffs, 2026-04-13 for regular season)."
  }
}

